STEP 3 -- the per-destination `facility` command                                PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: set `log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5` while the global setting stays
  local4, and capture both legs at the same time. The question is whether the
  per-destination command overrides the global one for that collector. Three windows
  were needed to answer it.

awplus(config)# log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5
awplus(config)# end
awplus# show running-config | include log
  log facility local4
  log host 10.38.22.33
  log host 10.38.22.33 level notices
  log host 10.38.22.33 facility local5          <-- the line under test
  log host 10.38.22.40
  log host 10.38.22.40 level notices
  log host 10.38.22.65
  log host 10.38.22.65 level notices

  Window 1 (170 s, both legs). Global local4, plus the per-host line on .33:
    eth3 (.33, carries `facility local5`):
      25 frames, 25 messages, by facility: local4(20)=25
      by PRI: 163=local4.err x6  165=local4.notice x19
      EXPECT facility local5(21): 0 of 25 message(s) match  !! 25 DO NOT      (exit 4)
    eth4 (.65, no per-host facility line):
      25 frames, 25 messages, by facility: local4(20)=25
      EXPECT facility local4(20): 25 of 25 message(s) match                   (exit 0)

  So the line changed nothing on the wire. The device's own configuration view says why:

awplus# show log config
    Host 10.38.22.33 log:
      Time offset .... +0:00          Offset type .... UTC
      Source ......... -              Secured ........ disabled
      Delay parameters:   Startup-Delay: 120    Buffered-Messages: 2000
      Filters:
      1   Level ...... notices    Program .... any    Facility ... any
          Msg text ... any        Type ....... include
      2   Level ...... any        Program .... any    Facility ... local5
          Msg text ... any        Type ....... include
      Statistics ..... 2295 messages received, 168 accepted, 0 excluded

  The command did not set a property of the collector. It added filter 2, in the same
  shape as the `Level notices` filter beside it. A filter chooses which messages to send.
  It does not rewrite a message.

  Window 2 (170 s, both legs). The global override removed, the per-host line kept:
awplus(config)# no log facility
awplus# show log config | include Facility:
  Facility: default
    eth3 (.33, still carries `facility local5`):
      15 frames, 15 messages
      by facility: user(1)=9  syslog(5)=6
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x8  43=syslog.err x2  45=syslog.notice x4
    eth4 (.65, no per-host facility line at all):
      15 frames, 15 messages
      by facility: user(1)=9  syslog(5)=6
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x8  43=syslog.err x2  45=syslog.notice x4

  The two collectors received exactly the same distribution. With nothing else in force,
  the per-host line still did not restamp a single message.

  Window 3 (170 s, both legs). The positive half: if the command is a selection filter,
  then a collector whose only filter is `facility user` must receive the user-facility
  messages and none of the syslog-facility ones.
awplus(config)# no log host 10.38.22.65 level notices
awplus(config)# log host 10.38.22.65 facility user
    eth3 (.33, filters: Level notices + Facility local5):
      13 frames, 13 messages
      by facility: user(1)=8   syslog(5)=5
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x7  43=syslog.err x2  45=syslog.notice x3
    eth4 (.65, filter: Facility user only):
      7 frames, 7 messages
      by facility: user(1)=7
      by PRI:      11=user.err x1  13=user.notice x6     <-- zero syslog-facility messages

  => STEP 3 PASS: the behaviour of `log host <ip> facility <f>` is now fully known. It is
  a message-selection filter on the facility a message was raised under. It is not an
  override and it never rewrites the PRI byte. The proof has three parts, each with its
  own control: with a global override in force it changed nothing (25 of 25 still
  local4); with no global override at all it changed nothing (the same distribution as a
  collector without the line); used as a filter it selects (7 of 7 user messages, 0
  syslog messages, while the other collector received 5 syslog messages in the same
  window). `show log config` is honest about this, because it lists the setting under
  `Filters:`. The requirement of this case is still met, by the global `log facility`
  command measured in STEP 2.

STEP 4 -- severity is not changed by the facility override                      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: a deliberately failed command gives an error-level message in the same window
  as the notice-level markers. Both must carry the overridden facility and must keep
  their own, different, severities. This was measured in the STEP 2 window.

  eth3, the same 25 messages as STEP 2:
      by severity: err(3)=6  notice(5)=19
      by PRI:      163=local4.err x6  165=local4.notice x19

    eth3  13:11:58.565777 v4 10.38.22.34:55161 -> 10.38.22.33:514  PRI=163
          local4.err    (fac 20, sev 3)  2026 Oct 22 00:11:42 awplus IMISH[4271]: Command [end] failed
    eth4  13:11:57.399788 v4 10.38.22.66:59360 -> 10.38.22.65:514  PRI=163
          local4.err    (fac 20, sev 3)  2026 Oct 22 00:11:42 awplus IMISH[4271]: Command [end] failed

  => STEP 4 PASS: two severities travelled under the overridden facility and each kept
  its own value. PRI 163 is 20*8+3 (err) and PRI 165 is 20*8+5 (notice). The override
  acts on the facility field only. STEP 5 widens this to four severities.

